md warns on stderr and then exits 0, the same defect as
list-fails-when-the-directory-does-not-exist, reached the other way: the path
exists but is a regular file.

`md list note.md` is an easy thing to type when you meant `md frontmatter --file
note.md`, and md answers it with silence on stdout and a 0 exit status. It must
exit non-zero.

Same cause: ListCommand.walkDirectory writes the warning and returns [], and
run() never learns that a directory could not be walked.
